{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "title": "Invoice",
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["id", "currency", "lines"],
  "properties": {
    "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^inv_[0-9a-f]{16}$" },
    "currency": { "type": "string", "enum": ["EUR", "USD", "GBP"] },
    "lines": {
      "type": "array",
      "minItems": 1,
      "items": { "$ref": "#/$defs/line" }
    }
  },
  "additionalProperties": false
}

type Query {
  """Look up a single order. Returns null if the caller lacks read:orders."""
  order(id: ID!): Order
  orders(first: Int = 20, after: String, status: OrderStatus): OrderConnection!
}

Pagination follows the Relay connection spec: pass the endCursor from pageInfo as after. Requesting first greater than 100 is a validation error rather than a silent clamp, because silent clamping made client-side paging loops run forever in an earlier version.

In the proto below, note that field 3 is reserved. We removed the legacy email field in v2 and reserved both its number and its name so nobody can reuse them and silently misinterpret old bytes on the wire.

message User {
  string id = 1;
  string display_name = 2;
  reserved 3;
  reserved "email";
  repeated string roles = 4;
}

Adding a field is always backward compatible; changing a field's type or number is not, even when the new type looks compatible.

location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://api_upstream;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    proxy_read_timeout 30s;
}

The trailing slash on both the location and the proxy_pass target matters: with it, /api/orders becomes /orders upstream; without it the /api prefix is preserved. Getting this wrong is the most common cause of a sudden wall of 404s after an nginx change.

## Installation

Requires Node 20 or later.

```
npm install --save @example/sdk
```

Then create a client and make your first call:

```js
import { Client } from '@example/sdk';
const client = new Client({ apiKey: process.env.EXAMPLE_API_KEY });
const me = await client.account.get();
console.log(me.plan);
```

Store the key in the environment, not in source control. If you are on a CI runner, add it as a masked secret so it does not appear in build logs.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowReadFromVPCEOnly",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::example-reports/*",
      "Condition": { "StringNotEquals": { "aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-0a1b2c3d" } }
    }
  ]
}

An explicit Deny always wins over any Allow, so this policy blocks object reads that do not arrive through the VPC endpoint even for administrators. Test it from a bastion outside the VPC before you rely on it.
